Myth: The Resonant Chord, once established, became the heartbeat of the firmament. However, the ancients knew that such harmony was not a permanent fixture, but a recurring gift. Boreas and Lyra understood that if the bridge remained static, it would eventually fade into commonality, losing its luster. Thus, they instituted the Law of the Cyclic Pulse. Every period of brilliant luminescence—the Aurora—had to be preceded by an intentional withdrawal. It was a test of faith, a deliberate act of letting go to see if the other would reach back across the void.

When the lights vanished, it was not because the Weaver had grown tired or the Giant had become cold; it was because they were extending invitations. Each cycle of darkness was a garden waiting for seeds of trust. If one party reached out, the other would respond, weaving the fabric of the night anew. This rhythm taught the mortals that true connection requires constant renewal. To love or to trust is to be willing to initiate the dance, knowing that the absence of light is merely the preparation for a greater, more vibrant display of shared destiny, forged not in certainty, but in the courage to offer oneself again.

